On Mar 28, 9:34=A0pm, karadoc <kara...@gmail.com> wrote:

> As I've said, I think the late game is a bit too easy. In particular I
> don't like that the hero is essentially immune to all the special
> attacks that the late-game monsters can deal. I think it's a shame
> that some of the late-game monsters essentially never get to use their
> special attacks. For example, wraiths and vampires are laughable
> because their level drain attack almost always fails. I also don't
> like that mc has some a massive affect but is not displayed anywhere
> in the game. So here's what I've changed it to:
>
> mc=3D0, p=3D100%
> mc=3D1, p=3D50%
> mc=3D2, p=3D25%
> mc=3D3, p=3D17%
>
> With this change, mc from armour is still very important but the
> difference is smaller between different armours. But more importantly,
> the best protection you can get is only a reduction to 17%, rather
> than 2%. So the nasty magic effects of monsters are much more likely
> to hurt you. This will have you backing away from wraiths and using
> ranged attacks rather than just steamrolling over them.
>

I think this is a very good idea. I've also thought that MC-3 is just
too much, for the reasons you give.

Another problem I have with MC is that it is very hard for non-spoiled
players to figure it out. I'm not sure what a good solution for that
would be. Essentially MC is a parallel "armor class" system (to
"special" attacks, rather than regular ones), but the player has
absolutely no indication of what is going on, either in the status bar
or in the intrinsics list.

Another way to make the late game a bit harder is to remove unihorn's
ability to restore stats, a la Unnethack. That used to drive me crazy
at first, but the more I've played, the more sense it makes. It makes
potions and spellbooks of restore ability actually worth something.

I remember an idea someone came up with years ago that I thought was
pretty good: make the fire and cold resistance intrinsics mutually
exclusive. That is, you can either be fire resistant (intrinsically)
or cold resistant (intrinsically), but not both. Getting one removes
the other. But you can still get the other resistance extrinsically.
This makes red or blue dragon scale mail a valid choice, and makes
fire/cold resistance rings considerably more valuable.

Also, nerfing poison resistance a bit might also make the late game
harder. It's always sort of bugged me that poison resistance
absolutely negates the danger of poison for the rest of the game. You
go from very concerned about poison to it being a complete non-issue.
But I'm not sure how to make the balance work. Poison resistance
should be still something you really want to get, but it shouldn't
remove every possible danger. Definitely no instadeaths for poison-
resistant characters tho.


